From 7588d822242698aacfef31aa60cb5e8f5830960e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 22:15:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] camera: known-limits table

Why: Core knows the fps a sensor mode can deliver (probe and table) but
nothing about what the recording path holds for a long take. That knowledge
exists only in the owner's test notes, so no screen and no rule can use it.
Dynamic resolution (the next commits) needs one place for these numbers.
Original Apertar keeps pure logic in a small header named after the concept,
std only, with one includer (StillBracketing.hpp:8-19@upstream-1.6.0), and
this repository host-tests such headers (FpsCeiling.hpp).

What: new src/camera/SustainedFps.hpp, with no caller yet. kCells holds one
row per measured or derived number: the fps a mode holds for a 30-minute take
on a hot drive, for the NVMe card and the USB SSD on a Pi 5 or CM5, per
picture size, Clear HDR or SDR, bit depth and compression, with its label
(measured, derived, probable, unconfirmed), the wall that limits it (back
end, encoder, drive, sensor), the short-take number where one was measured
and a source line. kPixelRates gives SDR 12-bit lossless crops a number.
limitFor finds a mode's row: its own cell, else the nearest larger cell of
its class, else the pixel rate, else none. capFor applies the levers (TDN
on stops an unbinned mode, phase lock and aids off cost a back-end mode one
fps each, split off on exFAT or FAT caps the fps at the flat-folder limit
of 32) to the number before stepDown rounds it down to a step: 25, 30, 33,
50, 60, 70, 80 and so on, 10 apart above 60. tools/sustained_fps_test.cpp
pins every number of the table, the step rounding, the lookup order, the
lever shifts and the flat-folder formula, so a change of the analysis has
to change the test too.

Tested: check_core.sh both builds and the host tests pass, with the new
test among them. Nothing calls the header yet, so no run on the camera is
needed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
